designs.checkpoint_and_resume:
THE LIVE CODEC IS ORDINARY TRAINED STATE. It goes into one payload key 'aud':
- encoder, router Wq/Wk, chunk projection and decoder weights;
- the OPT 'codec' group's AdamW moments, which never retire;
- the drift-probe reference (the last probe's upsampled latents and boundary flags, so the drift gauges continue across a resume);
- the readiness Gate's fired state.

Geometry, EXACT because tensors are shaped by these fields:
- aud.sr, aud.nfft, aud.hop (the analysis grid), aud.hid, aud.lat (chunk width 8), aud.dmax (duration head extent 16), lm.media_kmix (GMM components 8);
- aud.rate, 'fixed' or 'router' (router tensors exist only under 'router'; switching needs an R-CKPT 'born' rule).

MAY CHANGE across a resume: levers that set no shape (AUD_CHUNK_RATIO, AUD_STRIDE under 'fixed', the loss weights, the learning-rate floor).

aud.version stops being EXACT and becomes a reading: the codec step count plus a weight hash at save time. A live codec changes meaning continuously by design, so an EXACT version would refuse every resume.

NO ID WHOSE MEANING CHANGES IS PERSISTED ANYWHERE:
- media are re-encoded from raw clips at every window cut;
- MEM, DOM and EVAL store clip refs or waveforms, not codes or latents;
- the per-area media clip cursor goes into DATA stream_state.

The only co-adapted state is the LM's media input projection, GMM head and duration head, plus WORLD's media predictor. They are saved in the SAME checkpoint as the codec, so the saved pair is consistent by construction.

Resume is bit-exact if every piece is restored from one payload. The router's boundaries are a deterministic function of the weights (a threshold and a forced DMAX scan).

WHAT FAILS if pieces come from different checkpoints: measured analogue, stale P2 latents decoded by the P3 decoder, +0.063 / +0.076 mel. The CKPT load report must refuse a mixed-origin restore of aud versus lm.media_*.

The proposal's R-CKPT absent-field rule and add-a-modality resume carry over unchanged. The codec tensors, OPT 'codec' group and LM media head are BORN when AUD turns on over a text checkpoint.

WHAT GOES AWAY: the proposal's "old codes stay decodable (AQM)" guarantee. It is replaced by a MEASURED per-area codec-BWT row, because no old codes exist.

judge.rate_recommendation:
Stop treating the Hz as a property of the codec, and ship 25 Hz only as the measured default of a lever that can be changed without retraining.

(1) Build the codec on the 50 frames/s STFT grid as the one fixed time unit (the audio byte), with D3's nested quantiser. Each 80 ms segment is coded at stride 1, 2 or 4 (50 / 25 / 12.5 Hz), and one live codec decodes any stride map. Measured: at forced stride 2 it matched or beat the dedicated 25 Hz codec (s0: -2.6% tones and -4.7% melody bits/s, tones mel 0.444 vs 0.471). A pre-declared switch reverts to 'spec' if S3 does not reproduce this at 2 seeds.

(2) Ship AUD_RATE_MODE='fixed' with AUD_RATE_STRIDE=2 (25 Hz). At toy scale no adaptive route beat a fixed stride on the LM side:
- D3 'alloc' was +10.0% / +13.3% bits/s with table rows, and tied with coordinate rows (-2.1% / +2.6%, s0), with melody generation 1/42 vs 7/42;
- D1 BPE was +1.2 to +9.9 bits/s in 5 of 6 readings against fixed 50 fps;
- D2's router lost to a fixed stride at a matched average;
- forced 12.5 Hz cost +12% codec mel (0.481 vs 0.430).

(3) Build every adaptive route as an arm, because each works mechanically and shows content-dependent rates:
- 'alloc' (the teacher picks each segment's coarsest stride within (1+rho) of the finest reconstruction error; on the codec side it matched 25 Hz mel with 12-15% fewer positions);
- 'bpe' (TOK's mint mechanism over lattice ids, 34-43 positions/s by content from a 50 fps base);
- 'router' (H-Net, continuous route only).
'alloc' and 'bpe' change a clip's position count, so they need stage S0b (Q-RUN-8 option (a)); 'fixed' does not.

(4) Re-decide at S5 on GPU with 2-4 s clips, because 1 s clips can fake a low-rate cliff [S]. Use 2 paired seeds and matched compute, and sweep fixed 12.5 / 25 / 50, 'alloc' at rho 0.1 and 0.5, and 'bpe'. The default moves to an arm only if that arm wins the codec-invariant readings (generation and understanding exact, caption bits/byte, grounding bits) at both seeds without losing bits per audio-second beyond seed noise. On a tie the arm with fewer positions per second wins, because sequence length costs twice under card sharing. 12.5 Hz (Mimi's choice [V]) is a single lever flip with no codec retrain and must be in that sweep.

